"""Explicit coastal teaching content and original enquiry packages."""
from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':['Wave height is trough-to-crest vertical distance; wavelength is horizontal crest-to-crest distance; period is time between successive crests at one point.','A crest 1 m above and trough 1 m below a reference level give a 2 m wave height in the supplied symmetrical example.'],
'02':['Wind transfers energy to ordinary surface waves, with speed, duration and fetch influencing development.','Nearshore breaking transforms an existing wave and should not be substituted for its initial wind generation.'],
'03':['Swash moves water up the beach after breaking, whereas backwash returns water down towards the sea.','Direction must be interpreted from slope and land–sea orientation, not from a fixed direction on the page.'],
'03-01':['Oblique wave approach can produce diagonal upslope swash and move sediment.','A time sequence or direction evidence is needed to distinguish uprush from a still photograph of water.'],
'03-02':['Backwash returns down the slope under gravity and contributes to sediment redistribution.','The backwash direction differs from oblique uprush in the simplified longshore-drift model.'],
'04':['Constructive conditions have relatively stronger swash and tend towards beach accumulation; destructive conditions have relatively stronger backwash and tend towards beach removal.','A net upper-beach loss can coexist with offshore bar growth because sediment is redistributed between locations.'],
'04-01':['More sediment arriving than leaving a defined beach section gives net gain over the stated interval.','In the teaching example, 30 arriving units minus 18 leaving units gives a net gain of 12.'],
'04-02':['Relatively stronger returning transport can remove material from the upper beach under destructive conditions.','In the teaching example, 15 arriving units minus 28 leaving units gives net change of minus 13.'],
'05':['Marine abrasion wears a target with sediment, hydraulic action uses water force and solution chemically removes susceptible material.','Attrition wears the load through particle-to-particle collisions, distinct from abrasion of a cliff or platform.'],
'05-01':['Sand and shingle impacts can scrape or strike a coastal rock surface and remove material by abrasion.','Wind or waves alone do not establish abrasion without the appropriate sediment–target contact.'],
'05-02':['Wave pressure and water force acting on rock weaknesses can contribute to detachment by hydraulic action.','Trapped-air compression can contribute in some cracks, but its use needs suitable contextual evidence.'],
'05-03':['Marine solution erosion dissolves susceptible minerals under appropriate water-chemistry conditions.','Dissolved products provide stronger chemical evidence than surface smoothness alone.'],
'05-04':['Repeated collisions can reduce and round beach particles through attrition.','Rounded-particle appearance alone does not establish every stage of a particle’s transport history.'],
'06':['Coastal rock landforms develop through linked erosion, weathering and gravity under geological and exposure controls.','A supported formation sequence must fit the actual structure and viewing direction in the resource.'],
'06-01':['Cliff-base erosion can form a notch, remove support and contribute to collapse and retreat.','A wave-cut notch is a local basal recess, not the whole cliff or necessarily a deep cave.'],
'06-02':['A wave-cut platform is a relatively gently sloping rock surface left seaward as a cliff retreats.','Tidal exposure and possible sediment cover affect visibility; a sandy strip alone does not establish a rock platform.'],
'06-03':['Waves can enlarge a weakness into a cave within a cliff or headland.','A cave does not yet provide the through-passage beneath a roof that identifies an arch.'],
'06-04':['Cave enlargement and breakthrough can produce a passage through a headland beneath a surviving roof, forming an arch.','Two visible entrances require evidence of connection before proving one through-passage.'],
'06-05':['Arch-roof collapse can leave a detached rock pillar called a stack.','A stack’s separation from the mainland distinguishes it from a connected arch.'],
'06-06':['Continued erosion can reduce a stack to a low remnant called a stump.','Water-level changes affect stump exposure, so viewing conditions matter for interpretation.'],
'06-07':['Differential erosion can leave resistant rock projecting as headlands beside bays cut into less resistant material.','A headland can retreat while remaining relatively seaward because adjacent material retreats faster.'],
'07':['Oblique swash and more directly downslope backwash can produce net alongshore sediment movement called longshore drift.','Observed accumulation beside a groyne can support a drift interpretation when direction, supply and context are considered.'],
'08':['Sediment can move onshore and offshore as well as alongshore.','Upper-beach erosion and nearshore accumulation can represent redistribution rather than permanent loss from the whole system.'],
'09':['Beaches, spits, bars and tombolos differ in material accumulation and spatial connections.','Resource interpretation needs sediment type, land connections and water setting, not colour alone.'],
'09-01':['A beach is loose shore sediment, including sand or shingle, with a changing profile.','A visible waterline and exposed width depend partly on tide, so dated comparable references support change analysis.'],
'09-02':['A bay-mouth bar can connect across an opening, while an offshore bar lies seaward of the beach.','The word bar needs a stated geometric setting to distinguish these depositional forms.'],
'09-03':['A tombolo is a sediment connection between an offshore island or rock and the mainland.','Shelter and wave refraction around the offshore feature can favour connecting deposition under suitable conditions.'],
'09-04':['A spit is attached to land at one end with a free projecting tip and can form through longshore deposition beyond a coastal bend.','Namport identifies a sandspit sheltering Walvis Bay’s harbour, linking a depositional feature to port opportunity.'],
'10':['Hard structures and soft or land-use approaches address different coastal mechanisms and exposure problems.','Management needs an explicit criterion, conditions, time scale and wider sediment context.'],
'10-01':['Groynes can trap alongshore sediment locally but alter supply to downdrift reaches.','A wider updrift beach alone does not establish success for both neighbouring communities.'],
'10-02':['Seawalls reduce direct wave attack on assets behind them under their design conditions.','A wall does not replenish a narrowing beach or remove overtopping and all alternative flood pathways.'],
'10-03':['Gabions are rock-filled baskets whose performance depends on condition, stability and maintenance.','A low initial gabion cost does not establish low lifetime cost where repeated repairs are required.'],
'10-04':['Revetments are sloping protective structures that can dissipate wave energy along a frontage.','They require space and suitable foundations and may affect access or habitats.'],
'10-05':['Nourishment adds suitable sediment to a dynamic beach system and can widen its buffer and recreational surface.','Redistribution, source suitability and repeat funding influence performance; added sediment can move away from its placement point.'],
'10-06':['Land-use planning, setback and managed adaptation can reduce exposure without stopping waves.','Relocation and realignment can have social and livelihood costs and need support and planning.'],
'91':['Comparable beach profiles use fixed references, a defined transect and consistent segment distance/angle measurements.','Tide context, changing conditions and scale affect interpretation; one transect does not describe every reach.'],
'92':['Pebble sampling needs a stated selection rule, consistent axis measurement and a common roundness scale.','Size and shape are different variables, and a large sample does not cure biased selection.'],
'93':['Movement investigation needs a direction-aware reference, observation interval, repeated records and approved safe methods.','Non-recovery is not zero movement, and short-term drift evidence does not establish the annual regime.'],
'OBJ-6137-X.01':['Coastal features can support ports, tourism and habitats while creating erosion, flood and sediment-management constraints.','A named example needs a physical-feature-to-use link; changing statistics require dated sources.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Name the horizontal distance between successive wave crests.','Wavelength.',['1 mark for the name wavelength.']),
'002':('Describe the wave measurements: crest 1 m above reference water level, trough 1 m below; crests 12 m apart and passing one point every six seconds.','Height is 2 m from trough to crest. Wavelength is 12 m. Period is six seconds.',['1 mark for the 2 m height feature; 1 for the 12 m horizontal evidence; 1 for interpreting the six-second time measurement.']),
'003':('Describe how ordinary wind-generated surface waves develop.','Wind acts on the water surface and transfers energy. Continued interaction increases disturbances under suitable wind speed, duration and fetch. The resulting wave travels and may later transform and break near shore.',['1 mark for the first wind–surface interaction; 1 for continued energy transfer with relevant controls; 1 for travelling wave and later breaking as a distinct result.']),
'004':('Describe what this comparison supports: wind speed and duration are similar, but fetch is longer at A than B and larger waves are recorded at A.','The larger waves coincide with the longer fetch. Similar speed and duration make fetch a relevant changed control. The evidence supports a fetch contribution, not a conclusion that wave height depends on fetch alone.',['1 mark for the paired wave/fetch pattern; 1 for the stated-control evidence; 1 for a conditional interpretation.']),
'005':('Distinguish swash from backwash in a breaking-wave sequence.','Swash is the uprush up the beach after breaking; backwash returns down towards the sea. The difference is movement direction and stage, not two names for the same flow.',['1 mark for uprush swash; 1 for returning backwash; 1 for the explicit directional/sequence difference.']),
'006':('Suggest which movement is swash and which backwash: after breaking, A runs uphill on the beach; B returns downhill towards the sea. Give the evidence for each.','A is swash because it follows breaking and runs upslope. B is backwash because it returns downslope towards the sea under gravity.',['1 mark for applying swash to A; 1 for the given post-breaking/uphill reason; 1 for B’s return-direction evidence and backwash interpretation.']),
'007':('Describe the swash evidence: a time sequence shows breaking water then an uprush carrying a grain diagonally to higher beach positions.','The water moves uphill after breaking. Its diagonal path includes an alongshore component. The associated grain movement supports sediment transport by the observed swash.',['1 mark for the post-breaking uphill feature; 1 for the diagonal-path observation; 1 for the water–grain relationship.']),
'008':('Describe the backwash evidence: water retreats from a higher beach marker downslope towards the sea, carrying some grains lower.','The movement is returning water down the beach. Its direction is consistent with gravity-driven backwash. The grains show some seaward/downhill transport during that return.',['1 mark for the return-direction feature; 1 for using the higher-to-lower observation; 1 for the associated sediment movement.']),
'009':('Distinguish constructive and destructive wave conditions by swash/backwash balance and beach response.','Constructive conditions have relatively stronger swash and tend to accumulate beach material. Destructive conditions have relatively stronger backwash and tend to remove material from the beach section.',['1 mark for the relative swash/backwash difference; 1 for constructive accumulation; 1 for destructive removal, kept as tendencies under the stated conditions.']),
'010':('Suggest which of the two regimes in the supplied original figure is constructive and which destructive, using swash, backwash and profile change as evidence.','The regime with relatively stronger uprush and net beach build-up is constructive. The regime with relatively stronger return transport and net upper-beach removal is destructive. Offshore deposition can accompany the latter without contradicting upper-beach erosion.',['1 mark for the constructive application with evidence; 1 for the destructive application with reason; 1 for interpreting the stated profile change at the correct location.']),
'011':('Describe the net beach response: over one stated interval, 30 teaching units of material enter an upper-beach section and 18 leave it.','The section gains 12 units overall. More arrives than leaves. That local balance supports constructive behaviour during the interval, not proof of a permanent whole-coast trend.',['1 mark for the positive balance pattern; 1 for the 30−18=12 evidence; 1 for a properly limited constructive interpretation.']),
'012':('Describe the net response: 15 teaching units enter an upper-beach section and 28 leave; a nearshore bar gains material in the same interval.','The upper beach loses 13 units. Its removal pattern is consistent with destructive conditions there. The bar gain indicates possible offshore redistribution rather than proving all sand left the coastal system.',['1 mark for the negative upper-beach pattern; 1 for the 15−28=−13 evidence; 1 for interpreting the contrasting bar location.']),
'013':('Describe marine abrasion, hydraulic action and attrition by their main contacts.','Abrasion uses sediment against a fixed shore surface. Hydraulic action uses water force on rock or sediment weaknesses. Attrition wears transported particles through collisions with each other.',['1 mark for the abrasion contact step; 1 for direct water force; 1 for load-to-load contact and wear result.']),
'014':('Describe the processes supported: A shows shingle scraping a platform; B water pressure detaching a jointed block; C pebbles colliding and losing corners.','A supports abrasion of the platform. B supports hydraulic action. C supports attrition of the load.',['1 mark for A’s contact/target feature; 1 for B’s water-force evidence; 1 for C’s particle-collision relationship.']),
'015':('Describe abrasion evidence: shingle impacts a cliff face, scratches appear and fragments detach at the impact zone.','The moving load contacts the cliff. The scratches and detachment show wear of that target. This supports abrasion, not merely movement of grains nearby.',['1 mark for the load–target relationship; 1 for scratch/detachment evidence; 1 for the supported abrasion interpretation.']),
'016':('Describe hydraulic-action evidence: waves force water into a cliff joint; pressure changes precede loosening of a block; no sediment impact is recorded in that event.','Water acts directly on the weakness. The pressure/loosening sequence supports hydraulic action. The observation does not require an unrecorded abrasive collision.',['1 mark for the water/joint relationship; 1 for the pressure–loosening evidence; 1 for distinguishing the supported action from abrasion.']),
'017':('Describe solution-erosion evidence: water contacts carbonate rock and analysis detects dissolved mineral products leaving the face, without recorded scraping.','The change involves chemical removal of susceptible material. Dissolved products support corrosion/solution. The record differs from mechanical abrasion.',['1 mark for the chemical-removal feature; 1 for the dissolved-product evidence; 1 for the distinction from scraping.']),
'018':('Describe attrition evidence: marked shingle repeatedly collides with other shingle and loses corners, becoming smaller and rounder.','The contacts are between load particles. Projecting material is lost through collisions. The resulting smaller rounder form supports attrition of the load.',['1 mark for the particle-contact relationship; 1 for corner-loss evidence; 1 for the rounding result interpretation.']),
'019':('Describe the sequence linking a cliff-base notch to retreat and a wave-cut platform.','Base erosion deepens a notch and removes support. Overlying material collapses and debris is removed. Repeated retreat leaves a low rock surface seaward of the new cliff line.',['1 mark for the first notch/undercutting step; 1 for collapse and debris removal; 1 for retreat/platform as the result.']),
'020':('Describe the landforms and erosion sequence in the supplied original rocky-coast figure, using the connections between headland, cave, arch and isolated rock remnants.','Weakness enlargement produces a cave; breakthrough beneath a roof gives an arch. Roof collapse can leave a detached stack. Further reduction can leave a lower stump, with the resource showing different stages and connections.',['1 mark for the cave-to-arch connection feature; 1 for the roof-collapse/separation evidence; 1 for the stack-to-stump pattern where shown.']),
'021':('Describe the cliff/notch interpretation: an indentation at the cliff foot deepens, a supported overhang becomes unsupported and blocks later fall.','The basal recess is a wave-cut notch. Deepening removes support. Collapse contributes to cliff retreat in the supplied sequence.',['1 mark for the notch position feature; 1 for the support-loss evidence; 1 for the collapse/retreat relationship.']),
'022':('Describe platform evidence: a present cliff lies landward of an older recorded line, with a gently sloping rock surface exposed seaward at low water.','The cliff has retreated landward. The exposed rock strip lies in front of the newer cliff. Together these observations support a wave-cut platform, not simply a loose sandy beach.',['1 mark for the landward-change pattern; 1 for rock/low-water exposure evidence; 1 for the platform interpretation.']),
'023':('Describe cave evidence: a clear opening extends into a headland along a weakness but the surveyed chamber does not pass through the rock.','The opening follows a weakness into the headland. It forms a chamber rather than a through-passage. The evidence supports a cave, not yet an arch.',['1 mark for the weakness/opening feature; 1 for the surveyed chamber evidence; 1 for the cave–arch distinction.']),
'024':('Describe arch evidence: a surveyed passage connects openings on opposite sides of a headland beneath a surviving rock roof.','There is an open route through the headland. The roof remains above it. The connected opening supports an arch rather than two unconnected caves.',['1 mark for the through-passage relationship; 1 for the roof observation; 1 for the connection-based interpretation.']),
'025':('Describe stack evidence: an earlier arch loses its connecting roof, leaving a tall rock pillar separated from the mainland by water.','Roof collapse breaks the connection. The remaining pillar is isolated offshore. That sequence and separation support a stack.',['1 mark for the collapse pattern; 1 for the isolated-pillar evidence; 1 for linking the two to stack formation.']),
'026':('Describe stump evidence: a dated sequence shows an isolated tall stack progressively reduced to a low rock base that is exposed at low water.','The remnant becomes lower through continued reduction. It remains rock rather than a sand deposit. The low-water exposure and former-stack record support a stump.',['1 mark for the height-reduction pattern; 1 for rock/exposure evidence; 1 for the former-stack interpretation.']),
'027':('Describe the headland/bay pattern: relatively resistant A projects seaward, less resistant B is recessed, and both have retreated from an earlier straight coast.','A remains relatively seaward as a headland while B forms a bay. Greater retreat of B is consistent with lower resistance. A’s projection does not mean A has experienced no erosion.',['1 mark for the projection/recess pattern; 1 for the resistance/retreat evidence; 1 for retaining A’s documented retreat.']),
'028':('Describe the swash/backwash sequence that produces longshore drift.','Oblique waves send swash diagonally up the beach. Backwash returns more directly downslope under gravity. Repeated movements produce net sediment displacement along the shore.',['1 mark for the first diagonal-swash step; 1 for returning backwash; 1 for net alongshore movement as the result.']),
'029':('Describe the net direction in the supplied original plan: sea west, land east, northeastward swash and westward downslope return, repeated from a marked origin. Use its north arrow and sediment path.','Swash moves the grain northeastward up the beach and backwash returns it westward downslope. The repeated path retains a northward component, so net drift is northward for the observed interval.',['1 mark for the zigzag movement pattern; 1 for directional evidence from the north arrow/positions; 1 for the correct net drift interpretation in the final resource.']),
'030':('Describe how offshore movement can alter a beach and create a nearshore deposit.','Transport removes some material from the upper beach. It moves seaward within the nearshore zone. Where it accumulates, a bar can grow while the upper profile is reduced.',['1 mark for the first upper-beach removal step; 1 for seaward transfer; 1 for bar accumulation and profile contrast as the result.']),
'031':('Describe this cross-shore change: an upper profile loses material after energetic conditions while a submerged ridge farther seaward gains sediment.','The upper beach is reduced. The nearshore ridge gains material at a different position. The paired pattern supports offshore redistribution, without proving every grain’s destination.',['1 mark for the upper-profile loss feature; 1 for offshore-gain evidence; 1 for a qualified redistribution interpretation.']),
'032':('Describe a sequence by which a spit can develop beyond a bend in the coastline.','Longshore transport supplies sediment along the coast. Material continues beyond the bend where accumulation is possible. The deposit extends as a ridge attached at one end with a free tip.',['1 mark for the first alongshore-supply step; 1 for deposition beyond the bend; 1 for the attached/free-ended spit result.']),
'033':('Describe the differences between the beach, free-ended sediment ridge, bay-closing ridge and island connection in the supplied original depositional-coast resource.','The beach lies along the shore; the spit attaches at one end and projects freely; the bay-mouth bar connects across the opening; the tombolo links an offshore island to the mainland.',['1 mark for beach/spit form contrast; 1 for the bay-closing bar evidence; 1 for the island–mainland connection relationship.']),
'034':('Describe the beach evidence: loose sand and shingle form a belt along the shore, with an upper profile gaining material while a lower part loses it.','The loose shore accumulation is a beach. The stated profile change is uneven redistribution. It does not demonstrate uniform growth across the whole beach.',['1 mark for the loose-sediment shore feature; 1 for the upper/lower observation contrast; 1 for interpreting redistribution rather than uniform change.']),
'035':('Describe the bar interpretation: a sediment ridge joins the sides of a bay opening, leaving water behind it; a separate profile shows another ridge offshore with water between it and the beach.','The first is a bay-mouth bar and enclosed-water setting. The second is an offshore bar. Their different positions and connections distinguish two uses of the term.',['1 mark for the bay-closing feature; 1 for the offshore-separation evidence; 1 for the position-based contrast.']),
'036':('Describe tombolo evidence: a surveyed sediment ridge joins an offshore island to the mainland, with wave shelter around the connection.','The ridge connects island and mainland. Its sedimentary connection distinguishes it from a free-ended spit. Shelter is consistent with conditions favouring deposition in the link.',['1 mark for the island–mainland relationship; 1 for sediment-connection evidence; 1 for the qualified shelter/deposition interpretation.']),
'037':('Describe spit evidence: beyond a coastal bend, a narrow sand ridge is attached at its western end and projects freely eastward; the resource records eastward sediment transport.','The ridge has an attached end and free tip. Its extension beyond the bend aligns with the supplied transport. That pattern supports a depositional spit.',['1 mark for the attached/free-ended feature; 1 for bend/transport evidence; 1 for the spit interpretation.']),
'038':('Name the hard-engineering structure that extends across a beach to interrupt some longshore sediment movement.','A groyne.',['1 mark for the name groyne.']),
'039':('How far do you agree that one coastal defence can be judged successful from its presence alone? A council builds a structure, but records no beach change, condition inspections or neighbouring effects. Judge protection and wider sediment consequences.','Disagree. Presence shows installation, not its performance. Beach response and condition evidence are needed for local protection, while neighbouring observations are needed to assess sediment effects. The conclusion should remain open until monitoring addresses the stated objectives.',['Up to 4 C marks for judging the missing performance, condition and neighbouring evidence against the criterion; credit developed reasoning.','1 C mark for a supported decision that installation alone is insufficient proof.']),
'040':('How far do you agree that the groyne succeeds for both communities? Drift is northward; sand accumulates south of the groyne while the northern neighbouring beach narrows. Judge beach protection across both frontages.','Only partly agree. The southern accumulation is consistent with local trapping and benefit. Reduced onward supply is a plausible contribution to northern narrowing, so success for both is not established. Investigate wider causes and sediment management before extending the claim.',['Up to 4 C marks for the south/north evidence, trapping mechanism and qualified downdrift implication.','1 C mark for a supported judgement addressing both communities.']),
'041':('How far do you agree that a seawall alone meets both aims? It protects a road edge under the design conditions, but the recorded recreational beach continues narrowing. The aims are road protection and maintaining a wide beach.','Partly agree: direct road protection is supported, but maintaining beach width is not. A wall does not replenish the sediment budget and can have local effects. The two aims need separate performance evidence and potentially complementary management.',['Up to 4 C marks for judging the distinct road/beach evidence and mechanisms without assuming complete protection.','1 C mark for a supported partial-success decision against both aims.']),
'042':('How far do you agree that the cheapest initial gabion proposal is necessarily best over ten years? The exposed site has damaged baskets nearby and the proposal includes no repair funding. Judge sustained protection within total costs.','Disagree with necessity. Low initial cost omits likely maintenance needs suggested by the damaged-basket evidence. Exposure and unfunded repair could undermine performance, so lifetime cost and suitability need assessment before the proposal can be preferred.',['Up to 4 C marks for judging initial versus lifetime cost, condition/exposure evidence and the maintenance consequence.','1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting price alone as sufficient.']),
'043':('How far do you agree that a sloping rock revetment is automatically preferable to a seawall? It can dissipate energy but the site has little space and the proposed footprint occupies a valued access strip. Judge protection and usable space.','Disagree with automatic preference. Energy dissipation is a relevant benefit, but the footprint conflicts with limited space and access. Suitability depends on design and alternatives that address both aims; sloping form alone does not settle the decision.',['Up to 4 C marks for judging the energy benefit, footprint/access evidence and conditional design fit.','1 C mark for a supported decision using both criteria.']),
'044':('How far do you agree that one beach-nourishment programme is sufficient to maintain width without further spending? Suitable sand is added, but waves redistribute it and the plan has no future supply or monitoring budget. Judge sustained width.','Disagree. Nourishment adds sediment and can widen the beach, but redistribution is part of the system. Without monitoring or provision for repeat supply, sustained width is uncertain. Sand movement alone does not prove failure, but sustained width is not established.',['Up to 4 C marks for the sediment-input benefit, redistribution evidence and repeat-funding/monitoring limitation.','1 C mark for a supported judgement rejecting the guarantee while retaining the benefit.']),
'045':('How far do you agree that setback is the more direct way to avoid new coastal exposure here? The project is a new building; an alternative set-back site has services; the proposed shoreline site is exposed and assumes unfunded future defences. Judge future exposure of the new asset.','Agree substantially for this setting. The serviced set-back alternative avoids placing the new asset at the exposed edge, while the shoreline option relies on defences not funded. Setback does not stop erosion and still needs suitable planning, but it addresses the stated new-exposure problem more directly.',['Up to 4 C marks for applying the new-build, serviced-alternative and unfunded-defence evidence to exposure.','1 C mark for a supported decision with the distinction between reducing exposure and stopping erosion.']),
'046':('Describe the beach-profile procedure using the supplied equipment and transect resource at an approved safe site.','Start from a fixed reference on a defined shore-normal transect. Measure segment distances and slope angles with consistent pole heights. Record each signed angle and distance so the profile can be reconstructed on stated scales.',['1 D mark for the first fixed-reference/transect step; 1 for consistent segment measurement; 1 for the recorded distance/angle result.']),
'047':('Explain why repeated beach profiles should use the same fixed start and comparable transect direction.','Different starts or routes can sample different parts of the beach. Consistent references make an observed profile difference more interpretable as change rather than a measurement-location effect.',['1 D mark for the reason that location affects the profile; 1 for the consequence for a valid repeated comparison.']),
'048':('Describe a consistent pebble-selection and measurement procedure using the supplied beach sampling resource.','Specify positions by the approved selection rule. Apply the same rule for selecting a pebble and measure the agreed axis in consistent units. Record size and shape separately using the same roundness scale, with a stated rule for no suitable pebble.',['1 D mark for the first defined-selection step; 1 for consistent axis/units; 1 for the recorded size/shape result and unavailable-pebble rule.']),
'049':('Explain why pebble size and roundness should be recorded separately rather than inferring one from the other.','Size and shape are different variables: large stones can be rounded and small stones angular. Separate records allow the actual relationship to be tested instead of assumed.',['1 D mark for the reason distinguishing the variables; 1 for the consequence for testing a relationship.']),
'050':('Describe a beach-material movement investigation using the supplied approved tracer record and direction diagram; do not enter hazardous water.','Define a reference, direction system and observation interval. Record recovery or observed movement positions and repeat trials under the approved safe method. Keep unrecovered observations distinct from zero displacement when recording results.',['1 D mark for the first reference/time/direction step; 1 for repeated positional measurements; 1 for a result record distinguishing non-recovery from zero movement.']),
'051':('Explain why unrecovered tracers should not be assigned zero displacement.','Non-recovery means position is unknown, not that the material stayed still. Treating it as zero can bias the movement estimate and conclusion.',['1 D mark for the reason distinguishing unknown from zero; 1 for explaining the resulting bias risk.']),
'052':('Explain how a coastal sandspit can provide an opportunity and a related management constraint for a harbour.','A spit can shelter water from direct exposure, supporting harbour use, as Namport describes at Walvis Bay. Its changing sediment and connected transport create management questions, so shelter does not imply an unchanging or maintenance-free setting.',['1 A mark for the shelter factor; 1 for the link to harbour opportunity; 1 for a sediment-change constraint; 1 for its management effect without invented local rates.']),
'053':('Describe the opportunity and limitation in this resource: a sandspit shelters a harbour while dated surveys show changing sediment positions near access routes.','Shelter provides a physical benefit for harbour activity. The sediment-position changes show a dynamic setting. The paired evidence supports opportunity with a management constraint rather than permanent stability.',['1 B mark for the shelter/use relationship; 1 for the changing-sediment evidence; 1 for the qualified opportunity/constraint interpretation.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','03','05'],'prompt':'(a) Name the highest part of a wave. [1]\n(b) Describe abrasion, hydraulic action and attrition by their main contacts. [3]\n(c) Name the water movement returning down a beach. [1]\n(d) Describe two measurements from this supplied record: crest +1 m, trough −1 m relative to still water; crests 12 m apart. [2]','answer':'(a) Crest. (b) Sediment against shore surface; water force on weaknesses; particles colliding and wearing each other. (c) Backwash. (d) Trough-to-crest height is 2 m; wavelength is 12 m.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for the name crest.','(b) 3 A marks for the three distinct contact point descriptions, one per mechanism.','(c) 1 A mark for backwash.','(d) 2 B marks for interpreting the resource height and wavelength with correct units.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['04','07','09'],'prompt':'Original data: beach A receives 30 teaching units of sediment and loses 18; B receives 15 and loses 28. A plan records oblique northeastward swash and westward downslope return on a shore with sea west and land east.\n(a) Name the uprush movement after breaking. [1]\n(b) Describe the A/B net-change pattern and its constructive/destructive interpretation. [3]\n(c) Describe the net drift direction and the evidence supporting it. [2]\n(d) What judgement is supported about whether these short observations prove the annual drift regime? [1]','answer':'(a) Swash. (b) A gains 12 units and B loses 13; their local balances support constructive A and destructive B for the interval. (c) Repeated northeastward/westward movements leave a northward component, supporting net northward drift. (d) No: the short observation does not establish the year-round regime.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for swash.','(b) 3 B marks for A’s net gain, B’s net loss and the paired interpretation using the resource data.','(c) 2 B marks for northward net direction and correct use of the two component movements as evidence.','(d) 1 C mark for a supported decision recognising the limited period.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'B'),('c','Describe',2,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['07','09','10'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that the proposed groyne scheme meets both aims in the supplied original coastal-management map? The aims are maintaining the local recreational beach and not reducing sediment supply to the neighbouring reach. The hypothetical map records northward drift and comparable widths before→after: southern U 20→30 m, northern D 18→10 m, reference C 20→19 m. Use these data and the scheme evidence; judge both frontages. [5]','answer':'Only partial success is supported. Updrift U gains 10 m, consistent with local trapping, but downdrift D loses 8 m compared with a 1 m reference loss. Reduced onward supply is a plausible consequence, though other site differences need investigation. Local widening does not establish both aims, so full success across the neighbouring frontages is not demonstrated.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the actual drift, updrift/downdrift change and scheme evidence against both aims; the final map must support each credited point.','1 C mark for a supported overall decision on both criteria; independent review must verify the finished image and this rubric together.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]},
{'objectives':['03','07','91'],'prompt':'''A supplied safe survey compares beaches A and B. H1: A has a steeper overall surveyed profile than B. H2: A is wider than B at the comparable reference level. A profile data (distance m, height m): (0,0), (10,0.5), (20,1.5), (30,3). B: (0,0), (10,0.3), (20,1). Width repeats: A 30,32,31 m; B 20,22,21 m. The profile plot must use the supplied axes: distance 0–30 m, height 0–3 m. Use provided data; do not enter hazardous water.
(a) Name the instrument used to measure a beach-slope angle. [1]
(b) Describe the sequence producing longshore drift. [3]
(c) Name the two water movements in that sequence. [2]
(d) Calculate A’s overall gradient as a ratio with both distances in metres. [3]
(e) Describe the A/B profile pattern using values. [3]
(f) What conclusion about H1 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(g) What conclusion about H2 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(h) Design a repeatable profile procedure using the same reference conditions. [3]
(i) Describe how distance and angle readings would be recorded consistently. [3]
(j) Complete the field-record headings for the two variables needed for each profile segment, including units. [2]
(k) Plot A’s four supplied points on the provided axes. [2]
(l) Explain why comparable tide/reference conditions matter. [2]''','answer':'''(a) Clinometer. (b) Oblique swash moves material diagonally uphill; backwash returns downslope; repeated cycles produce net alongshore displacement. (c) Swash and backwash. (d) Vertical/horizontal = 3/30 = 0.1, equivalent to gradient 1:10. (e) A rises 3 m over 30 m; B 1 m over 20 m, making A steeper overall in these surveys. (f) H1 is supported by 1:10 versus 1:20; these are sampled profiles, not every place/date. (g) All supplied A widths exceed B, supporting H2 for the comparable measurements; the sample does not establish all-season widths. (h) Fixed start, defined shore-normal line and consistent pole/segment method under approved safe comparable conditions. (i) Record positions, distances and signed angles with consistent pole reference heights and units, retaining repeats or errors transparently. (j) Segment distance (m); slope angle (degrees). (k) Correct coordinates are (0,0),(10,0.5),(20,1.5),(30,3), connected in order. (l) Water level changes apparent width/reference positions, so comparable conditions reduce a timing/measurement confound.''','guide':['(a) 1 A for clinometer; (b) 3 A for the linked swash/backwash/net sequence; (c) 2 A for the two names.','(d) 3 B: method vertical/horizontal in equal units, 3/30 simplification, correct 1:10 ratio. (e) 3 B: A values, B values and the comparative interpretation.','(f) 3 C: supported hypothesis H1 conclusion, comparative gradient evidence and evaluation of sampled-profile limitations. (g) 3 C: supported hypothesis H2 conclusion, repeated width evidence and limited-period coverage.','(h) 3 D: reference/line, consistent equipment/segment procedure, approved safe/comparable conditions. (i) 3 D: traceable position record, consistent distance/angle procedure, signs/units/repeats.','(j) 2 D: one per correct variable heading with unit. (k) 2 D: accurate supplied-coordinate plotting and appropriate ordered connection on the supplied axes; no credit merely for rewriting the list. (l) 2 D: reason linking tide to width/reference and the resulting comparability.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-1.5-05: original blank profile axes and an answer-plot variant; pending Claude.']},
{'objectives':['05-04','92','93'],'prompt':'''A safe supplied pebble survey uses the same selection rule and three observations per site. H1: mean longest-axis size increases northward. H2: the proportion classed rounded increases northward. Sites at northward distances 0,10,20 m have sizes (18,20,22), (28,30,32), (38,40,42) mm. Rounded counts out of three are 0,1,0. Use a supplied roundness scale consistently. The plot axes are northward distance 0–20 m and mean size 0–50 mm.
(a) Name the erosion process involving load-particle collisions and rounding. [1]
(b) Describe its contact and change sequence. [3]
(c) Name two different pebble variables that must be kept separate. [2]
(d) Calculate mean size at the 0 m site. [3]
(e) Describe the size and rounded-count patterns. [3]
(f) What conclusion on H1 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(g) What conclusion on H2 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(h) Design systematic selection along the measured stretch, including a starting rule and no-suitable-pebble rule. [3]
(i) Describe how size and shape would be measured and recorded consistently. [3]
(j) Complete the headings for the measurement unit of size and the stated shape classification. [2]
(k) Plot mean size at all three sites on the supplied axes. [2]
(l) Explain why a larger sample alone does not fix biased selection. [2]''','answer':'''(a) Attrition. (b) Transported particles collide, corners/fragments are removed and particles can become smaller/rounder. (c) Size and roundness/shape. (d) (18+20+22)/3 = 20 mm. (e) Mean size rises 20→30→40 mm, while rounded counts rise then fall 0→1→0. (f) H1 is supported at these sites; source differences and the small/date-specific sample limit a broader claim. (g) H2’s steady increase is not supported; the middle peak and equal end counts contradict it, though three pebbles per site give weak wider coverage. (h) State a start and regular interval, apply an unbiased fixed local selection rule and predeclare treatment when no eligible pebble is present. (i) Measure the same axis/instrument/units, use the same roundness scale and record each result against position. (j) Longest-axis size (mm); rounded/not rounded according to the supplied scale. (k) (0,20),(10,30),(20,40). (l) Repeating a biased choice can increase the count while retaining systematic exclusion or preference.''','guide':['(a) 1 A for attrition; (b) 3 A for collision, corner loss and size/rounding result; (c) 2 A for size and shape.','(d) 3 B: correct total/three-observation method, 20 result and mm unit. (e) 3 B for the size trend with values, non-monotonic rounded counts and explicit contrast between variables.','(f) 3 C: supported decision, size evidence and evaluation of sample/source limitations. (g) 3 C: hypothesis H2 rejection conclusion, middle/end evidence and small-sample limitation.','(h) 3 D: start/interval, consistent eligible selection, predeclared absence rule. (i) 3 D: axis/units, shared shape scale, position-linked record.','(j) 2 D for correct size unit and shape heading/classification. (k) 2 D for accurate three-point plotting and clear trend representation on supplied axes. (l) 2 D for the reason that bias is systematic and its persistence despite more observations.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-1.5-06: supplied roundness reference and blank size-distance axes/answer variant; pending Claude.']},
{'objectives':['03','07','93'],'prompt':'''Supplied movement observations use an approved safe method, a fixed origin and equal two-minute intervals. Positive displacement is north. H1: mean recovered displacement is northward. H2: recovered displacement increases in every successive trial. Trials 1–5 record +2,+4,+1,−1 m, and not recovered. The plot axes are trial 1–5 and signed displacement −2 to +5 m; non-recovery is not a plotted zero.
(a) Name the net alongshore sediment-transport process. [1]
(b) Describe its swash/backwash sequence. [3]
(c) Name two variables needed to report displacement clearly. [2]
(d) Calculate the mean signed displacement of recovered observations. [3]
(e) Describe the recovered pattern and exception. [3]
(f) What conclusion on H1 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(g) What conclusion on H2 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(h) Design repeated movement observations with a defined reference and safe approved method. [3]
(i) Describe a record that distinguishes recovery, zero and unknown positions. [3]
(j) Complete the totals for recovered and unrecovered trials. [2]
(k) Plot recovered signed displacements on supplied axes, leaving non-recovery distinct. [2]
(l) Explain why replacing non-recovery with zero could mislead. [2]''','answer':'''(a) Longshore drift. (b) Oblique swash carries sediment diagonally up the beach; return backwash moves downslope; repetition produces net alongshore movement. (c) Distance and direction. (d) (2+4+1−1)/4 = +1.5 m, northward. (e) Three northward records and one southward record; values do not increase steadily. (f) H1 is supported by the positive mean among recovered observations; missing trial 5 and short coverage limit the inference. (g) H2 is not supported: +4 falls to +1 and then −1; unknown trial 5 cannot repair the documented decreases. (h) State origin/direction and equal duration, repeat the approved observation method and retain safety/environmental constraints. (i) Record trial/time, position or signed distance/direction and recovery status; zero is a known unchanged position while unknown is missing. (j) Four recovered, one unrecovered. (k) Plot (1,2),(2,4),(3,1),(4,−1); mark trial 5 as missing separately. (l) Unknown position could have moved either way; inventing zero changes the mean and may distort the decision.''','guide':['(a) 1 A for longshore drift; (b) 3 A for the linked sequence; (c) 2 A for distance and direction.','(d) 3 B: signed-sum method 6/4, +1.5 result, m/north direction. (e) 3 B for the positive majority, negative exception and non-increasing sequence using the data.','(f) 3 C: hypothesis H1 conclusion for recovered data, positive-mean evidence, evaluation of missing/short-period limitations. (g) 3 C: hypothesis H2 rejection conclusion, documented decreases and correct treatment of unknown trial.','(h) 3 D: fixed reference, equal repeated interval/method, safe approved constraints. (i) 3 D: trial-linked measurements, direction-aware values, separate recovery/zero/unknown record.','(j) 2 D for four recovered and one missing. (k) 2 D for correct signed plotting and distinct missing-data treatment on supplied axes. (l) 2 D for the unknown-versus-zero reason and its effect on estimates/conclusions.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-1.5-07: reference-direction diagram and blank signed-displacement axes/answer variant; pending Claude.']},
{'objectives':['03','07','10-01','91'],'prompt':'''A hypothetical groyne investigation compares a southern updrift reach U, northern downdrift reach D and a reference reach C. Repeated surveys use comparable tide/reference conditions. H1: U gains more width than C. H2: D does not narrow. Mean widths before→after are U 20→30 m, D 18→10 m, C 20→19 m. U’s three after readings are 29,30,31 m. Wave/tracer observations support northward transport. Use supplied before/after plotting axes: reach U,D,C and width 0–35 m.
(a) Name the uprush after wave breaking. [1]
(b) Describe how a groyne can affect longshore sediment supply. [3]
(c) Name the two water movements in the simplified drift sequence. [2]
(d) Calculate U’s percentage width increase using the before width as denominator. [3]
(e) Describe the before/after contrasts across the three reaches. [3]
(f) What conclusion on H1 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(g) What conclusion on H2 is supported, with a limitation? [3]
(h) Design a repeatable survey to test future width changes, including a suitable comparison. [3]
(i) Describe how to control or record tide/reference and transect differences. [3]
(j) Complete U’s mean after width and its unit from the three readings. [2]
(k) Plot the before/after widths for U,D,C on the supplied axes. [2]
(l) Explain why the reference reach helps but does not prove a unique cause. [2]''','answer':'''(a) Swash. (b) A groyne intercepts some alongshore material, encouraging updrift accumulation and potentially reducing onward supply. (c) Swash and backwash. (d) (30−20)/20×100 = 50%. (e) U gains 10 m, D loses 8 m, C loses 1 m. (f) H1 is supported by U +10 versus C −1, but reaches may differ in other controls. (g) H2 is rejected by D’s measured 8 m narrowing; the change is compatible with reduced supply but does not alone prove the groyne is its sole cause. (h) Fixed comparable transects on both sides and at a justified reference, repeated over relevant dates with a stated width datum. (i) Use the same fixed starts/directions and comparable level conditions, recording timing and wave/context differences. (j) (29+30+31)/3 = 30 m. (k) U bars 20/30, D 18/10, C 20/19 with a clear before/after distinction. (l) The reference indicates wider change, but local differences and other inputs can remain, limiting unique causal attribution.''','guide':['(a) 1 A for swash; (b) 3 A for interception, updrift effect and potential onward-supply effect; (c) 2 A for the names.','(d) 3 B: correct increase/before method, substitution 10/20×100 and 50% result. (e) 3 B for the three correctly interpreted changes.','(f) 3 C: supported hypothesis H1 conclusion, U/C evidence and evaluation of comparability limitations. (g) 3 C: hypothesis H2 rejection conclusion, D change and qualified causal interpretation.','(h) 3 D: spatial comparison, fixed datum/transects, repeat plan. (i) 3 D: location consistency, level/time handling, contextual record.','(j) 2 D for mean 30 and unit m. (k) 2 D for accurate paired plotting and distinguishable before/after presentation. (l) 2 D for the wider-change reason and remaining local-confound limitation.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-1.5-05 and VIS-6137-1.5-08: survey layout and paired-width plotting variant; pending Claude.']}
]
